Let LFD offer every dungeon a player has outlevelled. Scale up, never down.
Touches: src/server/game/DungeonFinding/LFGMgr.cpp
         LFGMgr::InitializeLockedDungeons(), LFGMgr::GetRandomAndSeasonalDungeons()

Why: player request -- "always show all dungeons in LFD, even ones you
outlevel or aren't high enough to queue for yet; always show all tiers of
random dungeon as well", refined to "visible but locked makes sense, i
basically want everything to scale up, not down (for now)".

So the rule is asymmetric, and deliberately so:

  BELOW your level (outlevelled)  -> unlocked and queueable.
  ABOVE your level                -> still listed, still locked.

A locked dungeon is not hidden: the client shows it greyed out with its
lock reason, which is exactly the "visible but locked" behaviour wanted.

Two gates had to be touched, which is why this covers two functions:

  1. InitializeLockedDungeons() builds the per-player lock map for the
     individual dungeon list. LFG_LOCKSTATUS_TOO_HIGH_LEVEL (the player
     outlevels the dungeon) is removed. LFG_LOCKSTATUS_TOO_LOW_LEVEL is
     kept exactly as it was.
  2. GetRandomAndSeasonalDungeons() builds the set of Random Dungeon
     entries offered at all -- entries missing here are absent, not
     locked. It filtered on both minlevel and maxlevel; only the maxlevel
     half is dropped, so outlevelled tiers stay on offer and tiers above
     the player still do not appear.

Outlevelling content is not a reason to lock it here: zone scaling already
re-bases open-world creatures and their rewards onto the player's own
level, and the same reasoning applies to a dungeon a group deliberately
chose to walk into. It is also what keeps the queue usable -- level
brackets slice an already small population into pieces that never fill.

Everything else still locks exactly as before: expansion, disabled
dungeons, raid and instance binds, seasonal availability, the Death Knight
starting quest, and the whole item/quest/achievement/gear-score access
requirement block. Expansion in particular is still enforced in (2), since
an account without the expansion genuinely cannot enter those maps.

--- a/src/server/game/DungeonFinding/LFGMgr.cpp
+++ b/src/server/game/DungeonFinding/LFGMgr.cpp
@@ LFGMgr::InitializeLockedDungeons(), the lockData else-if chain
             else if ((dungeon->minlevel > level && !sWorld->getBoolConfig(...)) || (...))
                 lockData = LFG_LOCKSTATUS_TOO_LOW_LEVEL;
-            else if ((dungeon->maxlevel < level && !sWorld->getBoolConfig(CONFIG_DUNGEON_ACCESS_REQUIREMENTS_LFG_DBC_LEVEL_OVERRIDE)) || (sWorld->getBoolConfig(CONFIG_DUNGEON_ACCESS_REQUIREMENTS_LFG_DBC_LEVEL_OVERRIDE) && ar && ar->levelMax > 0 && ar->levelMax < level))
-                lockData = LFG_LOCKSTATUS_TOO_HIGH_LEVEL;

@@ LFGMgr::GetRandomAndSeasonalDungeons()
             if ((dungeon.type == lfg::LFG_TYPE_RANDOM || (dungeon.seasonal && sLFGMgr->IsSeasonActive(dungeon.id)))
-                    && dungeon.expansion <= expansion && dungeon.minlevel <= level && level <= dungeon.maxlevel)
+                    && dungeon.expansion <= expansion && dungeon.minlevel <= level)
                 randomDungeons.insert(dungeon.Entry());
